            border: 1px solid #ccc;\n   196→            }\n   197→        }\n   198→    </style>\n   199→</head>\n   200→<body>\n   201→    <div class=\"container\">\n   202→        <header>\n   203→            <h1>Agentic Coding Research Report</h1>\n   204→            <div class=\"subtitle\">Daily updates on AI-assisted development tools • May 9, 2026</div>\n   205→        </header>\n   206→\n   207→        <main class=\"three-column-layout\">\n   208→            <!-- New Products Section (40%) -->\n   209→            <section class=\"new-products\">\n   210→                <h2>New Products</h2>\n   211→                <div class=\"no-content\">\n   212→                    No new products discovered in this update cycle.\n   213→                    <br><br>\n   214→                    <em style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Check back later for new releases in the agentic coding space.</em>\n   215→                </div>\n   216→            </section>\n   217→\n   218→            <!-- Whitelist Updates Section (35%) -->\n   219→            <section class=\"whitelist-updates\">\n   220→                <h2>Product Updates</h2>\n   221→\n   222→                <div class=\"item\">\n   223→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   224→                        <span class=\"product-name\">Claude Code</span> - v2.1.128 Release\n   225→                    </div>\n   226→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   227→                        Latest Claude Code release with random session colors, MCP tool counts, plugin zip support, console channels for API key auth, model picker cleanup, OTEL isolation improvements, and numerous bug fixes.\n   228→                    </div>\n   229→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   230→                        <span class=\"update-type release\">Release</span>\n   231→                        May 8, 2026\n   232→                        <span style=\"margin-left: 8px;\">\n   233→                            <a href=\"https://claudefa.st/blog/guide/changelog\" target=\"_blank\">Changelog →</a>\n   234→                        </span>\n   235→                    </div>\n   236→                </div>\n   237→\n   238→                <div class=\"item\">\n   239→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   240→                        <span class=\"product-name\">Cursor</span> - PR Review, Build Plan in Parallel, and Split PRs\n   241→                    </div>\n   242→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   243→                        New PR review experience with inline review threads, commits tab, and changes tab. Build in parallel from plans using async subagents. Split changes into PRs with quick action. Pin skills as quick actions. Context usage breakdown.\n   244→                    </div>\n   245→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   246→                        <span class=\"update-type feature\">Feature</span>\n   247→                        May 6, 2026\n   248→                        <span style=\"margin-left: 8px;\">\n   249→                            <a href=\"https://cursor.com/changelog\" target=\"_blank\">Changelog →</a>\n   250→                        </span>\n   251→                    </div>\n   252→                </div>\n   253→\n   254→                <div class=\"item\">\n   255→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   256→                        <span class=\"product-name\">Cursor</span> - Model Controls, Spend Management, and Usage Analytics\n   257→                    </div>\n   258→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   259→                        Enterprise admins get model access controls with granular allow/blocklists, soft spend limits with intelligent alerts, updated usage analytics tab filtering, and team marketplace updates.\n   260→                    </div>\n   261→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   262→                        <span class=\"update-type feature\">Feature</span>\n   263→                        May 6, 2026\n   264→                        <span style=\"margin-left: 8px;\">\n   265→                            <a href=\"https://cursor.com/changelog\" target=\"_blank\">Changelog →</a>\n   266→                        </span>\n   267→                    </div>\n   268→                </div>\n   269→\n   270→                <div class=\"item\">\n   271→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   272→                        <span class=\"product-name\">Cursor</span> - Cursor Security Review Beta\n   273→                    </div>\n   274→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   275→                        Cursor Security Review now in beta on Teams and Enterprise plans with Security Reviewer (checks PRs for vulnerabilities) and Vulnerability Scanner (scheduled scans of codebase). Customizable security agents.\n   276→                    </div>\n   277→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   278→                        <span class=\"update-type announcement\">Beta</span>\n   279→                        May 6, 2026\n   280→                        <span style=\"margin-left: 8px;\">\n   281→                            <a href=\"https://cursor.com/changelog\" target=\"_blank\">Changelog →</a>\n   282→                        </span>\n   283→                    </div>\n   284→                </div>\n   285→\n   286→                <div class=\"item\">\n   287→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   288→                        <span class=\"product-name\">Aider</span> - v0.56.0\n   289→                    </div>\n   290→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   291→                        Enables prompt caching for Sonnet via OpenRouter, 8k output tokens for Sonnet via VertexAI and DeepSeek V2.5, new /report command for GitHub Issues, --chat-language switch, and numerous bug fixes.\n   292→                    </div>\n   293→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   294→                        <span class=\"update-type release\">Release</span>\n   295→                        May 5, 2026\n   296→                        <span style=\"margin-left: 8px;\">\n   297→                            <a href=\"https://github.com/paul-gauthier/aider/releases\" target=\"_blank\">Release →</a>\n   298→                        </span>\n   299→                    </div>\n   300→                </div>\n   301→\n   302→                <div class=\"item\">\n   303→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   304→                        <span class=\"product-name\">Claude Code</span> - v2.1.126 Release - Project Purge & Enhanced Features\n   305→                    </div>\n   306→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   307→                        Added project purge command, broader skip-permissions, pasteable OAuth codes, skill activation telemetry, gateway model picker, Windows PowerShell detection, and security fixes.\n   308→                    </div>\n   309→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   310→                        <span class=\"update-type release\">Release</span>\n   311→                        May 1, 2026\n   312→                        <span style=\"margin-left: 8px;\">\n   313→                            <a href=\"https://claudefa.st/blog/guide/changelog\" target=\"_blank\">Changelog →</a>\n   314→                        </span>\n   315→                    </div>\n   316→                </div>\n   317→            </section>\n   318→\n   319→            <!-- Technical Insights Section (25%) -->\n   320→            <section class=\"technical-insights\">\n   321→                <h2>Technical Insights</h2>\n   322→\n   323→                <div class=\"item\">\n   324→                    <div class=\"insight-type\">Opinion</div>\n   325→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   326→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/5/y-combinator-openai-stake/\" target=\"_blank\">Y Combinator's 0.6% stake in OpenAI worth over $5 billion</a>\n   327→                    </div>\n   328→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   329→                        John Gruber reports that Y Combinator owns about 0.6 percent of OpenAI. At OpenAI's current $852 billion valuation, that's worth over $5 billion.\n   330→                    </div>\n   331→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   332→                        John Gruber (via Simon Willison) • May 5, 2026\n   333→                    </div>\n   334→                </div>\n   335→\n   336→                <div class=\"item\">\n   337→                    <div class=\"insight-type\">Technical</div>\n   338→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   339→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/4/granite-svg-pelican/\" target=\"_blank\">Granite 4.1 3B SVG Pelican Gallery</a>\n   340→                    </div>\n   341→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   342→                        IBM released Granite 4.1 family of LLMs (3B, 8B, 30B sizes) under Apache 2.0 license. Simon tested SVG generation capabilities across 21 different quantized variants and found results underwhelming.\n   343→                    </div>\n   344→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   345→                        Simon Willison • May 4, 2026\n   346→                    </div>\n   347→                </div>\n   348→\n   349→                <div class=\"item\">\n   350→                    <div class=\"insight-type\">Research</div>\n   351→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   352→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/3/claude-sycophancy/\" target=\"_blank\">Claude Mythos sycophancy study</a>\n   353→                    </div>\n   354→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   355→                        Anthropic's automatic classifier judged sycophancy by willingness to push back, maintain positions, give proportional praise, and speak frankly. Most conversations showed no sycophancy (9%), but spirituality (38%) and relationships (25%) were exceptions.\n   356→                    </div>\n   357→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   358→                        Anthropic (via Simon Willison) • May 3, 2026\n   359→                    </div>\n   360→                </div>\n   361→\n   362→                <div class=\"item\">\n   363→                    <div class=\"insight-type\">Tutorial</div>\n   364→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   365→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/2/sightings-feature/\" target=\"_blank\">Building wildlife sightings feature with Claude Code for web</a>\n   366→                    </div>\n   367→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   368→                        Simon built a new 'Sightings' feature for his blog on his phone using Claude Code for web. The feature displays wildlife photos from iNaturalist with search, filters, and RSS.\n   369→                    </div>\n   370→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   371→                        Simon Willison • May 2, 2026\n   372→                    </div>\n   373→                </div>\n   374→            </section>\n   375→        </main>\n   376→\n   377→        <footer>\n   378→            <a href=\"#\">Archive</a>\n   379→            <a href=\"#\">About</a>\n   380→            <a href=\"#\">RSS Feed</a>\n   381→            <a href=\"#\">GitHub</a>\n   382→            <br><br>\n   383→            Generated automatically from research data • Last updated: 2026-05-09\n   384→        </footer>\n   385→    </div>\n   386→</body>\n   387→</html>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
